WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.322 --> 00:00:01.446
- Good morning Believe Nation.

00:00:01.446 --> 00:00:04.693
Today's message is live your life.

00:00:04.693 --> 00:00:06.776
Over to you, Ben Affleck.

00:00:08.827 --> 00:00:11.303
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:11.303 --> 00:00:13.827
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:13.827 --> 00:00:17.327
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:18.355 --> 00:00:22.518
- If you could give advice
to your 25-year-old self,

00:00:22.518 --> 00:00:23.744
what would you say?

00:00:23.744 --> 00:00:26.437
- You know, I'd be inclined to say,

00:00:26.437 --> 00:00:28.787
watch out for this guy and
don't spend money on this

00:00:28.787 --> 00:00:31.791
and buy real estate right up until 2007

00:00:31.791 --> 00:00:33.507
and then dump it all, that kind of thing.

00:00:33.507 --> 00:00:35.065
- Oh, that's good advice.

00:00:35.065 --> 00:00:37.980
- But the truth is about
youth and experience

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.463
and success and failure

00:00:39.463 --> 00:00:41.779
is that you just have to go
live it and experience it.

00:00:41.779 --> 00:00:44.226
And if you don't, it doesn't
make you a mature adult.

00:00:44.226 --> 00:00:46.788
I think that you're
defined by how you rise

00:00:46.788 --> 00:00:48.148
more than how you fall.

00:00:48.148 --> 00:00:50.168
- One of the most important
lessons that I ever learned

00:00:50.168 --> 00:00:52.571
as a human being was from Jeff Bezos,

00:00:52.571 --> 00:00:54.522
and I've told this story a
couple of times on the channel.

00:00:54.522 --> 00:00:56.855
But the idea is to live
life with no regrets.

00:00:56.855 --> 00:00:59.568
Here was a guy who was having
massive success at his firm,

00:00:59.568 --> 00:01:01.993
where he was the youngest vice president

00:01:01.993 --> 00:01:03.053
ever at his company,

00:01:03.053 --> 00:01:05.772
and he left it, he threw it all away

00:01:05.772 --> 00:01:07.729
to start a business called Amazon,

00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:09.429
where people thought he was crazy.

00:01:09.429 --> 00:01:11.101
Who would ever buy books online?

00:01:11.101 --> 00:01:13.134
Why are you throwing away
this successful career

00:01:13.134 --> 00:01:14.923
to do this thing?

00:01:14.923 --> 00:01:17.753
And his answer was, he didn't
want to live with regret.

00:01:17.753 --> 00:01:18.926
He could live with it not working,

00:01:18.926 --> 00:01:20.485
but he did not want to live with regret.

00:01:20.485 --> 00:01:23.001
And so, he calls it, in
a very technical term,

00:01:23.001 --> 00:01:25.448
the regret minimization framework,

00:01:25.448 --> 00:01:27.460
where basically, whenever you're
afraid to make a decision,

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.281
fast forward your life to age 70,

00:01:29.281 --> 00:01:32.373
you're looking back or maybe
by the time we get there,

00:01:32.373 --> 00:01:35.795
90, 150, look back on your life,

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:40.210
say, "Am I going to regret
saying yes or saying no to this?"

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:42.141
And if you're going to regret it,

00:01:42.141 --> 00:01:44.256
then take the opposite action.

00:01:44.256 --> 00:01:45.089
Do it.

00:01:45.089 --> 00:01:46.340
And so, this really came into play

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:50.075
into my life when I was in
Paris and I was a tourist,

00:01:50.075 --> 00:01:52.400
and this girl came up and
asked me for directions.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.656
And I wanted to ask her out
and I was too afraid to do it.

00:01:54.656 --> 00:01:58.223
And I just pointed her in the
direction she wanted to go,

00:01:58.223 --> 00:01:59.802
and I didn't ask her out.

00:01:59.802 --> 00:02:01.935
And then, I took a picture
as she was walking across

00:02:01.935 --> 00:02:05.026
the bridge to the other
side of the street,

00:02:05.026 --> 00:02:07.193
and I didn't actually
get her in the picture.

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:09.316
I couldn't tell because it
wasn't digital at the time.

00:02:09.316 --> 00:02:10.420
And I put that picture,

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:11.253
I printed it off big,

00:02:11.253 --> 00:02:12.662
and I put it on my wall at home,

00:02:12.662 --> 00:02:13.959
put it in my bedroom,

00:02:13.959 --> 00:02:16.073
as a reminder not to live with regret,

00:02:16.073 --> 00:02:18.504
as a reminder that next time
that I get in that situation,

00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:21.935
where I want to do something
but I'm too afraid,

00:02:21.935 --> 00:02:25.935
to do it because the
alternative is not knowing.

00:02:27.185 --> 00:02:29.964
And you can deal with a
little bit of short term pain,

00:02:29.964 --> 00:02:30.826
maybe you get rejected,

00:02:30.826 --> 00:02:31.757
maybe it doesn't work out,

00:02:31.757 --> 00:02:33.862
maybe she laughs at you, whatever it is.

00:02:33.862 --> 00:02:35.684
You can deal with a little
bit of short term pain

00:02:35.684 --> 00:02:38.641
versus of lifetime of not knowing.

00:02:38.641 --> 00:02:40.252
And so, I had that picture on my wall,

00:02:40.252 --> 00:02:42.292
and people thought it was
this beautiful French picture,

00:02:42.292 --> 00:02:43.125
like yes.

00:02:43.125 --> 00:02:45.250
I didn't tell anybody the story behind it.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.587
And then, an opportunity
came later on that year

00:02:47.587 --> 00:02:48.900
where I had a chance to go work

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.331
at the dream company I
thought I wanted to work at

00:02:51.331 --> 00:02:54.190
versus being involved in
this startup biotech company

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:55.512
that wasn't making a lot of money

00:02:55.512 --> 00:02:56.923
and making 300 bucks a month.

00:02:56.923 --> 00:02:59.057
And that was part of what helped me decide

00:02:59.057 --> 00:03:02.030
to stick with the company
and not go get the job,

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.527
where I would be making 80, 100k a year,

00:03:04.527 --> 00:03:05.738
lots of opportunities,

00:03:05.738 --> 00:03:07.445
the thing that all my friends wanted

00:03:07.445 --> 00:03:08.995
and the company's flying me to New York,

00:03:08.995 --> 00:03:10.555
putting me up in fancy hotels,

00:03:10.555 --> 00:03:13.062
and wining and dining, all of that stuff.

00:03:13.062 --> 00:03:16.197
Saying no to do this small company

00:03:16.197 --> 00:03:18.504
that had a little chance of success,

00:03:18.504 --> 00:03:19.924
hadn't proven itself at all,

00:03:19.924 --> 00:03:21.995
I wasn't making any kind
of real money from it,

00:03:21.995 --> 00:03:23.308
why would I do that?

00:03:23.308 --> 00:03:26.498
Because I could live
with the company failing,

00:03:26.498 --> 00:03:29.215
but I couldn't live with not knowing.

00:03:29.215 --> 00:03:32.558
And so, I try to live my
life ever since that point

00:03:32.558 --> 00:03:35.353
of reducing as many regrets as possible.

00:03:35.353 --> 00:03:36.526
And every time I ask myself,

00:03:36.526 --> 00:03:37.974
am I going to regret this,

00:03:37.974 --> 00:03:40.221
as soon as I get an honest answer,

00:03:40.221 --> 00:03:41.701
then I know what I have to do.

00:03:41.701 --> 00:03:43.138
So, that's my story.

00:03:43.138 --> 00:03:44.357
And the question of the day today is

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:47.551
what advice would you give
your 25-year-old self,

00:03:47.551 --> 00:03:49.529
just like Ben Affleck did.

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:50.530
Leave it in the comments below.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:51.697
Super curious to find out.

00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:53.514
I also want to give a
quick shout out to Liliana.

00:03:53.514 --> 00:03:55.886
Liliana, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:03:55.886 --> 00:03:56.719
Your One Word.

00:03:56.719 --> 00:03:58.453
Really, really, really means a lot to me,

00:03:58.453 --> 00:03:59.896
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:03:59.896 --> 00:04:00.980
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.874
I hope you have an amazing day.

00:04:02.874 --> 00:04:03.863
I believe in you

00:04:03.863 --> 00:04:06.056
and hope you continue
to believe in yourself,

00:04:06.056 --> 00:04:08.809
and I'll see you again tomorrow
morning for another shot

00:04:08.809 --> 00:04:09.993
of Entspresso.

00:04:09.993 --> 00:04:12.436
♫ I wake up every morning

00:04:12.436 --> 00:04:16.019
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:04:18.602 --> 00:04:20.824
- We've only this one life.

00:04:20.824 --> 00:04:23.272
And we should make the best of it.

00:04:23.272 --> 00:04:25.022
That's what I try to.

00:04:26.594 --> 00:04:31.580
To do the best is not to
enjoy each day as the last,

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.695
celebrating, drinking, whatever

00:04:33.695 --> 00:04:35.669
because tomorrow it could be the end.

00:04:35.669 --> 00:04:39.589
No, no, no, it's to
make it a better place.

00:04:39.589 --> 00:04:42.915
I think it's only possible
in your surrounding.

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:45.182
You come in a room, try,

00:04:45.182 --> 00:04:48.208
let the people don't feel
worse when you come in.

00:04:48.208 --> 00:04:49.570
So, that's what I try to do,

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:52.352
that's how I understand life.

00:04:52.352 --> 00:04:53.967
- I think a lot of times you think that

00:04:53.967 --> 00:04:55.883
the great writers are the people,

00:04:55.883 --> 00:04:57.387
they just write hit after hit after hit.

00:04:57.387 --> 00:04:59.102
And they do, but it's also because

00:04:59.102 --> 00:05:01.990
they get through all the
terrible songs first,

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.238
and I think that's kind of
every single artist story.

00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:07.916
And I love to tout,

00:05:07.916 --> 00:05:09.889
when people ask what's your best advice

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:11.930
for an upcoming musician or anybody,

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:14.045
I think it's that you have to be able

00:05:14.045 --> 00:05:17.120
to rise from the failures is
what makes a successful person

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.427
because everybody's going to have mishaps,

00:05:19.427 --> 00:05:20.263
they're going to fall down,

00:05:20.263 --> 00:05:23.726
and it's the people that
continuously pick themselves up

00:05:23.726 --> 00:05:26.219
that are the ones that are
going to find their voice,

00:05:26.219 --> 00:05:28.048
make it, whatever you want to call it.

